    "reason": "The assigned POS for both English 'almost' and Japanese '大体' is 'verb', which results in an 'exact' match of the assigned labels. However, this is grammatically incorrect for both. 'Almost' is an adverb in English. '大体' when used to mean 'almost' is also an adverb. This fundamental mislabeling of POS in the input data needs correction. Semantically, '大体' can mean 'almost' or 'nearly', but its JMdict glosses also include 'generally', 'on the whole', 'mostly', 'approximately', and 'roughly', making it substantially broader than the specific meaning of English 'almost' (very nearly). While '大体' is a natural and common word in Japanese, 'ほとんど' (hotondo) is often a more precise and direct translation for 'almost' when referring to degree or completion.",
